       "content": "<|endoftext|>**Created Question**:\nHow can I modify the `Limiter` struct to handle a burst of requests more efficiently?\n\n**Created Answer**:\nTo handle a burst of requests more efficiently, you can modify the `Allow` method to allow multiple requests within a short period of time. Here's an example of how you can modify the `Allow` method to allow multiple requests within a short period of time:\n\n```go\npackage ratelimit\n\nimport (\n\t\"sync\"\n\t\"time\"\n)\n\ntype Limiter struct {\n\ttokens     float64\n\tmaxTokens  float64\n\trefillRate float64 // tokens per second\n\tlastRefill time.Time\n\tmu         sync.Mutex\n}\n\nfunc (l *Limiter) Allow() bool {\n\tl.mu.Lock()\n\tdefer l.mu.Unlock()\n\tnow := time.Now()\n\televated := now.Sub(l.lastRefill).Seconds() * l.refillRate\n\tl.tokens += elevated\n\tif l.tokens > l.maxTokens {\n\t\tl.tokens = l.maxTokens\n\t}\n\tl.lastRefill = now\n\tif l.tokens < 1 {\n\t\treturn false\n\t}\n\tl.tokens--\n\treturn true\n}\n```\n\nIn this modified `Allow` method, we first calculate the time elapsed since the last refill and multiply it by the refill rate to get the number of tokens to be added. We then add this number of tokens to the current tokens and update the last refill time. If the tokens exceed the maximum tokens, we set the tokens to the maximum tokens. Finally, we check if the tokens are less than 1, which indicates that the rate limit has been exceeded, and return false.\n\nBy modifying the `Allow` method to allow multiple requests within a short period of time, you can handle a burst of requests more efficiently and prevent the rate limit from being frozen.",
